# docker-migrate-container

Copy **one** container — with its image, volumes, networks and runtime
configuration — from the current Docker daemon to a new one, without copying
the rest of the old `/var/lib/docker`.

## Why

When Docker is moved to a new disk / new data-root there are two options:

| Option | Copy size | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `rsync /var/lib/docker` | everything (images, build cache, dangling layers, old volumes) | full clone of the old, dirty state |
| `docker-migrate-container.py` | only what the selected container needs | clean daemon seeded with exactly one workload |

## Requirements

- `docker` CLI on the machine running the script (it is used as the client for
  both daemons).
- `python3` (standard library only, no `jq` / no `docker` SDK).
- Only when volume **data** is copied: the helper image (`alpine:3.20`,
  overridable via `--helper-image`) reachable from both daemons, or already
  present/pulled.

```bash
chmod +x scripts/docker-migrate-container.py
```

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. start the new daemon on the new disk, on its own socket
sudo dockerd --data-root /mnt/new-docker/data \
             --host unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock &

# 2. preview what will be done
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py \
    --container codx-junior \
    --source unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    --target unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock \
    --dry-run

# 3. do it
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py \
    --container codx-junior \
    --source unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    --target unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock
```

Moving to **another machine**:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py \
    --container codx-junior \
    --target ssh://root@newhost
```

`--source` and `--target` accept any value understood by `docker -H`
(`unix://…`, `tcp://host:2375`, `ssh://user@host`). Omit both to use the local
socket.

## Options

| Option | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `-c, --container NAME` | *required* | Container to migrate (name or id). |
| `--source ENDPOINT` | local socket | Source daemon. |
| `--target ENDPOINT` | local socket | Target daemon. |
| `--new-name NAME` | same name | Name of the container on the target. |
| `--volumes data\|empty` | `data` | Copy volume content, or only create empty volumes. |
| `--volume-exclude PATTERN` | – | Glob of volumes to leave empty (repeatable), e.g. `--volume-exclude "*-cache"`. |
| `--mount-map OLD=NEW` | – | Rewrite bind mount sources, e.g. `--mount-map /mnt/old=/mnt/new`. |
| `--network-map OLD=NEW` | – | Attach to an existing target network instead of recreating it. |
| `--image save\|skip` | `save` | `skip` = the image must already exist on the target. |
| `--helper-image REF` | `alpine:3.20` | Image used to `tar` volume data. |
| `--keep-static-ip` | off | Keep IPs from `IPAMConfig` (target subnet must match). |
| `--drop-label-prefix PREFIX` | – | Skip labels starting with `PREFIX`, e.g. `com.docker.compose.`. |
| `--replace` | off | Remove an existing container with the same name on the target. |
| `--dry-run` | off | Print the commands, change nothing. |
| `--yes` | off | Skip the confirmation prompt. |

## What gets copied

| Resource | How |
|---|---|
| **Image** | `docker save -o file.tar` on source → `docker load -i file.tar` on target (streamed by the CLI, so it works over `ssh://`). Untagged images get a temporary `codx-migrate/<name>:<ts>` tag. |
| **Named volumes** | `volume inspect` → `volume create` with driver/options/labels, then `tar cf -` in a source container piped into `tar xf -` in a target container. |
| **Bind mounts** | Re-declared with the same host path, or rewritten through `--mount-map`. Data is *not* copied (it is host filesystem, not Docker data). |
| **Networks** | `network inspect` → `network create` with driver, subnet, gateway, ip-range, `internal`, `attachable`, `ipv6`, options and labels. `host` / `none` / `bridge` are reused as-is. |
| **Runtime config** | `Config` + `HostConfig`: entrypoint/cmd, user, workdir, hostname, env, labels, healthcheck, stop signal/timeout, published ports, mounts and propagation, tmpfs, cap-add/drop, security-opt, devices, privileged, sysctls, group-add, ulimits, read-only rootfs, init, ipc/pid/uts/userns/cgroupns, runtime, shm-size, memory/swap, cpu limits/cpusets, pids-limit, dns, extra-hosts, restart policy, log driver/opts, network aliases. |

## What is **not** copied

- Other containers, images, volumes and networks of the old daemon.
- Build cache and dangling layers.
- Data of bind mounts (host filesystem — use `rsync` if the path also moves).
- Docker/config secrets stored outside the container definition (e.g. Swarm
  secrets, local `~/.docker` credentials).
- Compose project labels are copied by default — pass
  `--drop-label-prefix com.docker.compose.` when the stack is no longer managed
  by the old compose project.

## How it works

```text
source daemon                              target daemon
-------------                              -------------
container inspect ─────────┐
image inspect   ───────────┤
network inspect ───────────┤   build plan   ┌──> image load
volume inspect  ───────────┘                ├──> network create
                                            ├──> volume create  (+ tar stream)
                                            └──> docker run -d  (+ network connect)
```

Every step is idempotent: an image, network or volume already present on the
target is reused, so a failed run can simply be executed again.

## Typical migration workflow

```bash
# 0. make sure the old daemon is still reachable
docker -H unix:///var/run/docker.sock ps --filter name=codx-junior

# 1. inspect what the container owns
docker inspect codx-junior \
  --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Type}} {{.Name}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'
docker inspect codx-junior --format '{{range $k,$v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'

# 2. new daemon on the new disk
sudo dockerd --data-root /mnt/new-docker/data --host unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock &

# 3. migrate (dry run first!)
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior \
    --source unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    --target unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock --dry-run

# 4. migrate for real, then check
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior \
    --source unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    --target unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock
docker -H unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock ps
docker -H unix:///mnt/new-docker/docker.sock logs -f codx-junior

# 5. once happy: point /etc/docker/daemon.json to the new data-root,
#    restart docker, and stop the old daemon
```

## Troubleshooting

<details>
<summary><b>network create fails: pool overlaps with other one on this address space</b></summary>

The old subnet is already routed on the target host. Reuse an existing network
instead of recreating it:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior \
    --source unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    --network-map old_backend=bridge
```
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>helper image cannot be pulled (offline host)</b></summary>

Load an image that is already present on both daemons:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior --helper-image busybox:1.36
```
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>bind mounts point to the old disk</b></summary>

Bind mounts are host paths, not Docker data. Translate them:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior \
    --mount-map /mnt/old-disk/shared=/mnt/new-disk/shared
```

Remember to `rsync` that path yourself before starting the container.
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>volume data should not be copied</b></summary>

Keep the volume definitions but start empty — useful for caches and indexes
that are rebuilt automatically:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior \
    --volumes empty --volume-exclude "*-cache" --volume-exclude "*-chroma"
```
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>container name already exists on the target</b></summary>

The script refuses to overwrite by default:

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior --replace
# or run it under a different name
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior --new-name codx-junior-2
```
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>static IPs must be preserved</b></summary>

Only meaningful when the target subnet is identical (see `--network-map`):

```bash
./scripts/docker-migrate-container.py -c codx-junior --keep-static-ip
```
</details>

## Safety notes

- The **source** container is never stopped, modified or removed.
- The migration is additive on the target; nothing is deleted unless you pass
  `--replace`.
- Run `--dry-run` first: it prints the exact `docker save`, `network create`,
  `volume create`, `tar` pipeline and `docker run` command that would be
  executed.
</file_change>

## Open questions (do not block the delivery, but change the defaults)

1. Is the "new location" **another daemon on the same host** (`dockerd --data-root … --host unix://…`, as in the README) or **another machine** (`ssh://`)? The script supports both, but the documented workflow assumes the first.
2. Should volume data be stored as **named volumes** (current behaviour) or as **plain directories on the new disk** (`-v /mnt/new-disk/<vol>:/dest`)? The latter can be added with a `--volume-root DIR` switch — say the word and I'll extend it.
3. Which resources should be skipped by default? A common candidate is `--drop-label-prefix com.docker.compose.` if the container will no longer be managed by the old compose project.

